n went ahead  and render them transparence we went  ahead and did tissue clearing with the  Saito veste 3d cell culture tissue clear  reagent and then went ahead to imaging  and we showed here is that higher self  seeding densities resulted in larger  more irregularly shaped steroids and we  looked at quantifying  Daffy and fixable dead cell stain what  we see here is a higher higher  concentration of cells that are dead at  the center of these models with a higher  seating density so essentially we're  doing here is quantifying the necrotic  core of these models and showing how  that's related to the seating density  the reason this is important is until  recently with the advent of tissue  clearing and applying it to 3d cell  culture models a lot of researchers just  simply thought there was nothing on the  interior of 3d cell culture models and  what we've shown by using tissue  clearing here is that we can actually  quantify what's going on within the  product cooler that we can do is very  simple and easy to use axes that are  rapid and easy to implement in any lab  and then lastly the third case study  here is looking at Hep C 2 cell  perforation so very common assay that a  lot of researchers might be running for  self information on different types of  cancer cell lines in this case we're  looking at taxol at varying  concentrations and looking at what  happens during the dosage of taxol  different concentrations so here we did  a pretreatment for 20 minutes we labeled  with ki-67 again for cell perforation we  then went ahead again and cleared the  models with the site Oh Vista three cell  culture tissue clearing reagents and  image and the significance of this is  the difference between clearing the  models and not clearing we're at a  control group here we didn't clear the  same models that were exposed to taxall  and we ran these dose response curves  looking at ki-67 and what we've shown is  there's rheumatic difference between  tissues that have been cleared and  tissues that have been not cleared and  the reason for that is when we're  looking at tax all and the non cleared  models essentially what we're doing is  we're only looking at cells on the  periphery of these models we're not  looking at the entire population of  cells within these 3d cell culture  models but when we clear the models we  are so we see a dramatic drop in the ki  67% of positive cells within these  models when we clear the models compared  to just looking at the non cleared  models because we're actually  characterizing the entirety of the model  and not just the periphery so this has  very large implications for drug  screening and the application of tissue  clearing actually allows us to  completely characterize the model  instead of biasing our results to just  the exterior and then one of the things  that we do a lot of is spatial dose  profiling so in this case here we took  these three cell culture models these  Hep C two models we broke them down into  inner middle and outer cross-sections  and for each one of those sections we  looked at ki-67 percent of positive  cells and this is by processing our data  as these stacks instead of looking at Z  projections this allows us to actually  look at cells within each one of those  layers and determine dose response  curves for each one of those this is  very important for profiling large  molecules is very important for seeing  how our little tiny models are actually  responding to the compounds and that  different components within those models  were actually responding to the  compounds over time  now I appreciate the opportunity to  present about the characterization of 3d  cell culture models if you have any  questions please feel free to reach out  at any time  my email is michael johnson at visit  call comm again I appreciate the  opportunity to speak with you today  thank you dr. Johnson for your  presentation a quick reminder for our  audience on how to submit questions  simply type them into the drop down box  located on the far left of your  presentation window labeled ask a  question and click on the send button  dr. Johnson will answer as many  questions as time permits first question  is what is the biggest challenge into  designing a 3d cell culture assay  I would say our biggest challenge is  that we work as a contract research  organization so researchers come to us  and say we have this specific problem we  like an assay that does this it has  these types of endpoints the biggest  challenge for us is the communication  with the researcher around what is the  minimum amount of data that's required  because for example if the researcher  wants to look at their model at 40 X  magnification the amount of data we're  going to generate at 40 X compared to 4  X is about thousandfold more so that  provides a lot of challenges for data  processing for analysis and just only  the throughput of the assay is very low  and the cost is going to be very high so  what we spend a lot of time on is work  with researchers to figure out what is  the minimum amount of data you need to  actually answer your research question  because if we try and acquire too much  data the cost of the assay is going to  make it prohibitive so this is somebody  spend a lot of time on is how do we  design the assay that provides us with a  rich amount of information to answer our  research question but also not too much  information that the throughput is very  low and the cost is very high so I would  say that's our biggest focus point is  figuring out what is that just minimum  that of data that we need I think the  next one is how deep into a 3d cell  culture model can you image with high  content confocal microscopy so this will  depend on the imaging system that you  have and the type of plate that you're  using the most ideal situation is using  an inverted confocal microscope which is  traditional for these assays and looking  on a glass bottom well plate with that  setup you can get to about a millimeter  in depth depending upon the model we  have image several models a millimeter  or deeper but I would say have  millimeters typically where most  researchers will get to with their  standard error objectives on their  microscope that is provided that you  have a laser based excitation source I  would say with an LED based excitation  source again it depends upon the model  but you'll probably be limited to about  200 to 250 microns and depth into the  model this can be slightly improved if  you use dipping objectives but with  tissue clearing it's not going to be  that dramatic of a difference between  using dipping objectives and air  objectives with a  3d cell culture models I would say  safely probably about a millimeter  depending upon the system that you're  using thank you how do your tissue  processing and labeling steps differ  with different types of models one of  the things that we've seen recently is  that a lot of researchers have been  working with neuronal models and we  found this very interesting because we  do a lot of work on holdout imaging of  brain tissues so for instance we'll look  at one millimeter thick sections of a  mouse brain we can image all the way  through they're on a high content  confocal system with our cx-7 lzr  but we notice quickly that with neuronal  serial-in models they are incredibly  dense much dancer than just regular  mouse brain tissue it actually is quite  challenging to label an image the rohtul  models compared to liver models or  several other types of cancer models  that we've looked at so for something  like that that's very dense the  incubation time of an antibody for  example the dilution that we're using is  going to be quite different than it  would be for a liver model so it really  depends on the type of model but for  very dense models like the neuronal ones  you do have to change your processing  steps you might just have to extend  steps for longer you might have to add  more triton you might have to change the  way the tissue is processed but it's  really going to depend on a case-by-case  basis we see a huge amount of diversity  and the types of models that researchers  are working with and what we typically  suggest is always spending upfront time  optimizing the tissue clearing and  labeling process before trying to dive  into developing and executing a  full-blown assay so you can actually  demonstrate that you're achieving  uniform labeling prior to actually  trying to implement an assay so instead  with upfront optimization any model can  be processed and labeled but it does  take time and it will differ depending  upon the type of model that you're  working with thank you your next  question is when is tissue clearing  beneficial for 3d cell culture assays  and when is it not  right so I think it's very important to  be transparent about this and about 3d  cell culture models in general 3d cell  culture models are useful for some  applications but for other applications  they're too expensive and not that  useful so for when tissue clearing is  beneficial it's the same thing if we're  trying to characterize a 3d cell culture  model and its entirety tissue clearing  is very beneficial for use with confocal  microscopy if you're doing widefield  microscopy again tissue clearing is  going to be very helpful to improving  the signal improving the number of cells  that are being characterized and  improving the accuracy of your assay but  outside of those 2 imaging based  applications tissue clearing isn't going  to be that beneficial if you're doing a  simple dissolution based assay of course  tissue clearing is not going to provide  you with that much utility but for any  type of imaging assay of a 3d cell  culture model I'd highly recommend using  tissue clearing to improve the number of  cells that you're characterizing and  unbiased in your data through actually  characterizing the whole population of  cells thank you looks like we have time  for one more question how do we process  optical v slices from a 3d cell culture  model this is a good question so I would  say until recently most research has  always used Z projections to process  their three-dimensional data and I think  this is one of the reasons why a lot of  researchers never really noticed per se  this bias around characterized in the  periphery of their models because if you  think of the projection of all of your Z  slices and you crunch them all together  it looks like you're characterizing the  entire model and you don't really see  those Center Z slices where you only  characterize the periphery that's what  most researchers have done because  limitations of that approach and the  inability to get spatial information  we've started to move away from that and  actually process the individual Z slices  the way we do that is we export the TIFF  images from our cx-7 lvr and we've  written a series of pipelines in image j  and cellprofiler to actually process  those individual Z slices so that's  typically our pros doing it is  processing it on our own and wiring as  much data as we possibly can for our  specific types of research projects  that is on a case-by-case basis the  projects that we do are very diverse in  nature based on the research question  and it really depends on what  specifically a researcher is asking for  in regards to why we use these slices  versus Z projection so it depends but  most of that work we've done an image J  and cellprofiler I would like to once  again thank dr. Johnson for his  presentation do you have any final  comments on the only comment I have is  so I appreciate the opportunity to speak  today I really appreciate lab roots and  thermo Fisher Scientific allowing us  this opportunity if there are any  questions feel free to reach out we're  very interested in collaborating with  researchers and helping out any way we  can thank you I'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their interesting questions questions we  did not have time for today will be  addressed by the speaker via the contact  information you provided at the time of  registration we would like to thank our  sponsor Thermo Fisher Scientific for  underwriting today's educational webcast  this webcast can be viewed on-demand  through February 2019 lab routes will  alert you via email when it's available  for replay we encourage you to share  that email with your colleagues who may  have missed today's live event until  next time goodbye
 

